  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1007 AM MDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nA similar set-up to previous days is expected for today. The \nsubtropical high remains over AZ/NM with weak E-NE flow around it\nand not much forcing. Scattered convection develops over area \nmtns early in the afternoon, drifting into the lowlands by the \nevening. Large DCAPE values and steep lapse rates will result in \ngusty to strong outflow winds to 50 mph. Blowing dust is a concern\nagain for the Lordsburg Playa, likely less of a risk than the \nlast few days. Heavy downpours are possible as well with PWs at or\nslightly above normal for early August. Areas W of the Divide \nhave the highest threat of flash flooding today, but is marginal. \nIsolated spots of 1-2\" are possible out west through the evening.\nStorm chances lessen as you go south and east with a slight chance\nof showers/storms for the Las Cruces area late this evening.\n\nThe upper high drifts northwest for Thu, bringing in some added \nmoisture from the east (PWs 1.1-1.2\"). Storm coverage looks to \nincrease for the lowlands as a result with a slightly higher risk\nof heavy rainfall, focusing over SW NM. Strong outflow winds to \n50 mph and blowing dust are possible again on Thu for southern \nNM. Mostly dry conditions prevail for far west TX. \n\nNot much movement in the upper high is modeled into next week, \nsitting close to the Four Corners. Storm chances trend down for Fri \nand into the weekend. Area mtns and western areas will remain \nfavored for storms with mainly dry conditions in the lowlands to end \nthe period. Global ensembles are hinting at the monsoon high making \na new home over the Gulf Coast later next week, which would ramp up \nour monsoon flow and rain/storm chances. PWs look to reach around \n1.25\" at KELP after Wed according to the LREF. This pattern is \nsomething to monitor into next week.\n\nTemps stay warm to hot into next week as the high remains close by. \nThe lower RGV will be near advisory criteria through Thu with a \nslight cooldown into the weekend while the high migrates a bit \nfurther away. Temps climb a touch early next week, but will \ngenerally be about 5 degrees above normal. Lowland highs stay near \n100 or just above into next week. Winds remain light from the E-SE\neach day outside of outflow boundaries.",
